1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,747
What if everything you can see, touch and remember is running on a machine you will never find?

2
00:00:06,747 --> 00:00:07,558
Not a dream.

3
00:00:07,558 --> 00:00:08,233
A program.

4
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:14,029
Elon Musk once said the chance we live in base reality is one in billions.

5
00:00:14,029 --> 00:00:17,197
Millions of people take that idea seriously.

6
00:00:17,197 --> 00:00:20,366
Some say the programmers are not even human.

7
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:23,750
So today we board a train through time.

8
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:25,545
Seven stops, seven eras.

9
00:00:25,545 --> 00:00:29,060
At every stop, someone asked the same question.

10
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:32,799
And at the last stop, scientists tried to test it.

11
00:00:33,266 --> 00:00:36,337
The ticket says: are we living in a simulation?

12
00:00:36,337 --> 00:00:39,082
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

13
00:00:39,082 --> 00:00:41,500
And a verdict at the end of the line.

14
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:46,700
First stop.

15
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:50,033
Athens, about two thousand four hundred years ago.

16
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,632
The philosopher Plato wrote a scene that still feels modern.

17
00:00:54,632 --> 00:00:58,696
Prisoners chained in a cave since childhood, facing a wall.

18
00:00:58,696 --> 00:01:00,899
They can never turn their heads.

19
00:01:01,366 --> 00:01:03,133
Behind them burns a fire.

20
00:01:03,133 --> 00:01:10,414
Between the fire and the prisoners, people walk past carrying objects, like puppeteers behind a screen.

21
00:01:10,414 --> 00:01:13,099
The objects throw shadows on the wall.

22
00:01:13,566 --> 00:01:17,532
The prisoners hear echoes and think the shadows are speaking.

23
00:01:17,532 --> 00:01:20,783
For them, the shadows are not pictures of reality.

24
00:01:20,783 --> 00:01:21,889
They are reality.

25
00:01:21,889 --> 00:01:24,099
The only one they have ever known.

26
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:26,518
Then one prisoner is freed.

27
00:01:26,518 --> 00:01:28,614
The firelight hurts his eyes.

28
00:01:28,614 --> 00:01:31,216
Dragged outside, the sun blinds him.

29
00:01:31,216 --> 00:01:38,299
Slowly he sees reflections in water, then real things, then the stars, and finally the sun itself.

30
00:01:38,766 --> 00:01:40,858
He goes back to tell the others.

31
00:01:40,858 --> 00:01:44,912
But his eyes cannot adjust to the dark, and they laugh at him.

32
00:01:44,912 --> 00:01:48,900
Plato says they would kill anyone who tried to drag them out.

33
00:01:49,366 --> 00:01:52,627
It sounds like a description of a virtual world.

34
00:01:52,627 --> 00:01:56,974
Shadows projected for an audience that cannot see the projector.

35
00:01:56,974 --> 00:02:00,166
Some simulation fans call it the first warning.

36
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:02,052
But check the label.

37
00:02:02,052 --> 00:02:07,939
Plato wrote this in his Republic to show the effect of education on the human mind.

38
00:02:07,939 --> 00:02:10,918
The cave is about knowledge and ignorance.

39
00:02:10,918 --> 00:02:12,266
Not about machines.

40
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:13,580
Second stop.

41
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:16,966
China, the fourth century before the common era.

42
00:02:17,433 --> 00:02:20,658
A philosopher named Zhuangzi had a dream.

43
00:02:20,658 --> 00:02:25,849
He was a butterfly, fluttering around, happy, doing as he pleased.

44
00:02:25,849 --> 00:02:28,366
He did not know he was Zhuangzi.

45
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:30,084
Then he woke up.

46
00:02:30,084 --> 00:02:32,352
Solid, unmistakable Zhuangzi.

47
00:02:32,352 --> 00:02:34,228
But now he was not sure.

48
00:02:34,228 --> 00:02:37,982
Was he a man who had dreamed he was a butterfly?

49
00:02:37,982 --> 00:02:41,266
Or a butterfly, now dreaming it was a man?

50
00:02:41,733 --> 00:02:44,655
He called it the transformation of things.

51
00:02:44,655 --> 00:02:48,969
And it asks the question every simulation argument depends on.

52
00:02:48,969 --> 00:02:52,866
From the inside, how could you ever tell the difference?

53
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:55,159
Think about your own dreams.

54
00:02:55,159 --> 00:02:58,225
While you are in one, it feels completely real.

55
00:02:58,225 --> 00:03:01,356
You only notice it was a dream when you wake up.

56
00:03:01,356 --> 00:03:03,900
If you never wake up, you never notice.

57
00:03:04,366 --> 00:03:05,380
Third stop.

58
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:05,933
India.

59
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:09,204
Indian philosophy has a word for this.

60
00:03:09,204 --> 00:03:09,573
Maya.

61
00:03:09,573 --> 00:03:12,525
In Sanskrit it means magic, or illusion.

62
00:03:12,525 --> 00:03:19,166
At first it was the power of a god to make people believe in something that was not there.

63
00:03:19,633 --> 00:03:28,103
Around thirteen hundred years ago, the philosopher Shankara made it central to the school called Advaita Vedanta.

64
00:03:28,103 --> 00:03:31,101
One reality, called Brahman, unchanging.

65
00:03:31,101 --> 00:03:33,500
The many things we see are maya.

66
00:03:33,966 --> 00:03:36,660
The classic example is a rope at dusk.

67
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:37,794
You see a snake.

68
00:03:37,794 --> 00:03:38,999
Your heart races.

69
00:03:38,999 --> 00:03:43,110
Then someone brings a lamp, and the snake was never there.

70
00:03:43,110 --> 00:03:45,166
It was a rope the whole time.

71
00:03:45,633 --> 00:03:47,894
An older verse says it directly.

72
00:03:47,894 --> 00:03:54,466
As a rope in the dark is imagined to be a snake, so the true self is imagined as many things.

73
00:03:54,933 --> 00:03:58,274
For simulation fans, this is the closest match.

74
00:03:58,274 --> 00:04:04,033
A whole world that feels solid, sitting on top of a deeper reality we cannot see.

75
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:05,993
But notice the detail.

76
00:04:05,993 --> 00:04:12,576
In Advaita the illusion always needs something real underneath it, like the rope under the snake.

77
00:04:12,576 --> 00:04:15,291
Nobody here is talking about a computer.

78
00:04:15,291 --> 00:04:17,666
It is about the nature of the self.

79
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:19,052
Fourth stop.

80
00:04:19,052 --> 00:04:19,511
Egypt.

81
00:04:19,511 --> 00:04:23,033
But we arrive in December nineteen forty-five.

82
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:30,021
A farmer named Muhammed al-Samman was digging for soft soil to fertilise his fields.

83
00:04:30,021 --> 00:04:31,963
He hit a sealed clay jar.

84
00:04:31,963 --> 00:04:34,835
He was afraid a jinn might be inside.

85
00:04:34,835 --> 00:04:36,233
He hoped for gold.

86
00:04:36,699 --> 00:04:38,347
Instead he found books.

87
00:04:38,347 --> 00:04:45,152
Twelve leather-bound papyrus volumes and part of a thirteenth, about sixteen hundred years old.

88
00:04:45,152 --> 00:04:50,166
Some pages were reportedly burned as kindling before experts saw them.

89
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:52,932
This is the Nag Hammadi library.

90
00:04:52,932 --> 00:05:00,333
Fifty-two texts, most of them Gnostic, from early Christian groups whose own writings were almost lost.

91
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,586
Their story is startling.

92
00:05:02,586 --> 00:05:06,159
The world we live in was not made by the true God.

93
00:05:06,159 --> 00:05:09,660
It was built by a lesser craftsman, the demiurge.

94
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:12,733
In some texts he is ignorant, even hostile.

95
00:05:13,199 --> 00:05:18,521
And humans are trapped inside his creation, with a spark from a higher world.

96
00:05:18,521 --> 00:05:20,042
The way out is gnosis.

97
00:05:20,042 --> 00:05:20,733
Knowledge.

98
00:05:21,199 --> 00:05:26,752
Swap the demiurge for a programmer and you get a story that sounds like The Matrix.

99
00:05:26,752 --> 00:05:30,966
A false world, a hidden maker, and an escape through knowledge.

100
00:05:31,433 --> 00:05:33,368
But again, check the label.

101
00:05:33,368 --> 00:05:37,381
The Gnostics were talking about God, evil and salvation.

102
00:05:37,381 --> 00:05:40,176
Their maker is a myth, not an engineer.

103
00:05:40,176 --> 00:05:41,681
A beautiful parallel.

104
00:05:41,681 --> 00:05:42,899
Not a prediction.

105
00:05:43,366 --> 00:05:44,176
Fifth stop.

106
00:05:44,176 --> 00:05:46,165
Holland, sixteen forty-one.

107
00:05:46,165 --> 00:05:47,933
A Frenchman by the fire.

108
00:05:48,399 --> 00:05:52,333
René Descartes wanted to find one thing he could not doubt.

109
00:05:52,333 --> 00:05:54,000
So he doubted everything.

110
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,066
He wrote that there are no sure signs to tell being awake from being asleep.

111
00:05:59,533 --> 00:06:02,041
Then he imagined something darker.

112
00:06:02,041 --> 00:06:06,761
A powerful demon, using all its skill to feed him a false world.

113
00:06:06,761 --> 00:06:08,826
Sky, earth, colours, sounds.

114
00:06:08,826 --> 00:06:09,933
All of it fake.

115
00:06:10,399 --> 00:06:14,504
In the twentieth century philosophers upgraded the demon.

116
00:06:14,504 --> 00:06:19,688
A brain in a vat, wired to a computer that sends it a perfect fake life.

117
00:06:19,688 --> 00:06:23,433
Hilary Putnam made it famous in nineteen eighty-one.

118
00:06:23,899 --> 00:06:26,302
Descartes found his one certainty.

119
00:06:26,302 --> 00:06:27,998
I think, therefore I am.

120
00:06:27,998 --> 00:06:31,955
Even a demon cannot fake the fact that you are thinking.

121
00:06:31,955 --> 00:06:34,499
But everything else stayed in doubt.

122
00:06:34,966 --> 00:06:38,863
Bertrand Russell later gave the common-sense answer.

123
00:06:38,863 --> 00:06:42,311
A life-long dream is not logically impossible.

124
00:06:42,311 --> 00:06:46,433
But a real world outside us is the simpler explanation.

125
00:06:46,899 --> 00:06:47,668
Sixth stop.

126
00:06:47,668 --> 00:06:49,066
The machines arrive.

127
00:06:49,533 --> 00:06:56,586
In nineteen sixty-nine, the German computer pioneer Konrad Zuse published Calculating Space.

128
00:06:56,586 --> 00:07:01,033
His idea: the universe itself might be a kind of computer.

129
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:07,950
The physicist John Wheeler went further, and described physics as the flow of information.

130
00:07:07,950 --> 00:07:08,810
It from bit.

131
00:07:08,810 --> 00:07:13,899
The roboticist Hans Moravec wondered if future minds might simulate us.

132
00:07:14,366 --> 00:07:20,950
Then, in two thousand three, an Oxford philosopher named Nick Bostrom published a short paper.

133
00:07:20,950 --> 00:07:23,751
Are you living in a computer simulation?

134
00:07:23,751 --> 00:07:26,133
It changed the whole conversation.

135
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:29,638
Bostrom did not say we are in a simulation.

136
00:07:29,638 --> 00:07:33,171
He said at least one of three things must be true.

137
00:07:33,171 --> 00:07:36,633
That structure is called the simulation argument.

138
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:37,764
Door one.

139
00:07:37,764 --> 00:07:44,633
Almost every civilization like ours dies out before it gets the technology to simulate minds.

140
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:45,736
Door two.

141
00:07:45,736 --> 00:07:52,733
Civilizations that do get the technology almost never choose to run simulations of their ancestors.

142
00:07:53,199 --> 00:07:53,995
Door three.

143
00:07:53,995 --> 00:07:57,395
We are almost certainly living in a simulation.

144
00:07:57,395 --> 00:08:03,833
Because if simulations are run at all, simulated people would vastly outnumber real ones.

145
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:05,787
His numbers are wild.

146
00:08:05,787 --> 00:08:11,664
He estimated all of human mental history at about ten to the thirty-six operations.

147
00:08:11,664 --> 00:08:18,533
A planet-sized computer could run that using less than one millionth of its power for one second.

148
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,462
And a simulation would not need to fake everything.

149
00:08:22,462 --> 00:08:27,078
Bostrom wrote that the inside of the Earth could be safely left out.

150
00:08:27,078 --> 00:08:30,133
Detail only has to appear when someone looks.

151
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:33,496
Here is where the alien theory comes in.

152
00:08:33,496 --> 00:08:36,755
Bostrom's simulators are our own descendants.

153
00:08:36,755 --> 00:08:42,114
But some believers say the programmers could be any advanced civilization.

154
00:08:42,114 --> 00:08:44,866
Aliens running Earth as an experiment.

155
00:08:45,333 --> 00:08:47,148
And Bostrom's own advice?

156
00:08:47,148 --> 00:08:54,628
In the dark forest of our ignorance, he said, split your belief roughly evenly between the three doors.

157
00:08:54,628 --> 00:08:56,371
Not ninety-nine percent.

158
00:08:56,371 --> 00:08:57,533
About one third.

159
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:58,781
Final stop.

160
00:08:58,781 --> 00:08:59,349
The lab.

161
00:08:59,349 --> 00:09:02,333
Can physics actually catch the programmer?

162
00:09:02,799 --> 00:09:04,593
Test one, twenty twelve.

163
00:09:04,593 --> 00:09:10,647
Physicists Silas Beane, Zohreh Davoudi and Martin Savage asked a simple question.

164
00:09:10,647 --> 00:09:14,833
If space is computed on a grid, could we see the pixels?

165
00:09:15,299 --> 00:09:19,418
The best place to look is the most energetic cosmic rays.

166
00:09:19,418 --> 00:09:22,958
A grid would make them prefer certain directions.

167
00:09:22,958 --> 00:09:27,582
Their paper set a limit on how tiny the pixels would have to be.

168
00:09:27,582 --> 00:09:30,399
So far, no grid pattern has been found.

169
00:09:30,866 --> 00:09:31,530
Test two.

170
00:09:31,530 --> 00:09:43,700
At Fermilab near Chicago, a two point five million dollar machine called the Holometer used two laser beams forty metres long to listen for a jitter in space itself.

171
00:09:44,166 --> 00:09:49,346
After a year of data, in December twenty fifteen, the answer came back.

172
00:09:49,346 --> 00:09:50,075
No jitter.

173
00:09:50,075 --> 00:09:55,328
One model of a pixelated universe was ruled out at four point six sigma.

174
00:09:55,328 --> 00:09:56,933
Others are still open.

175
00:09:57,399 --> 00:09:59,930
Test three is a lesson in headlines.

176
00:09:59,930 --> 00:10:09,000
In twenty seventeen, an Oxford paper showed some quantum effects cannot be efficiently simulated by one standard computer method.

177
00:10:09,466 --> 00:10:13,440
The internet called it proof that we are not in a simulation.

178
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:15,329
The paper never claimed that.

179
00:10:15,329 --> 00:10:19,433
It was about the limits of one method, on our kind of computer.

180
00:10:19,899 --> 00:10:21,670
Test four is still fresh.

181
00:10:21,670 --> 00:10:32,933
In twenty twenty-five, a team including Mir Faizal and Lawrence Krauss used Gödel's incompleteness theorem to argue the universe cannot be a simulation at all.

182
00:10:33,399 --> 00:10:35,518
Critics replied within weeks.

183
00:10:35,518 --> 00:10:39,172
Undecidable means you cannot prove certain things.

184
00:10:39,172 --> 00:10:41,948
It does not mean a program cannot run.

185
00:10:41,948 --> 00:10:47,866
Simple simulations already contain undecidable questions, and they run just fine.

186
00:10:48,333 --> 00:10:49,313
And the odds?

187
00:10:49,313 --> 00:10:55,874
Astronomer David Kipping ran the argument through Bayesian statistics in twenty twenty.

188
00:10:55,874 --> 00:11:00,926
His result: slightly less than fifty percent that we are simulated.

189
00:11:00,926 --> 00:11:02,133
Not ninety-nine.

190
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:04,149
The critics are blunt.

191
00:11:04,149 --> 00:11:07,179
Sabine Hossenfelder calls it pseudoscience.

192
00:11:07,179 --> 00:11:09,997
Frank Wilczek asks the hardest question.

193
00:11:09,997 --> 00:11:15,633
If this world is simulated, what is the computer made of, and whose laws run it?

194
00:11:16,100 --> 00:11:17,040
End of the line.

195
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:18,333
Here is the scorecard.

196
00:11:18,799 --> 00:11:23,487
Ancient thinkers doubted the senses: evidence, the texts survive.

197
00:11:23,487 --> 00:11:28,967
Ancients described a computer simulation: no evidence, those were metaphors.

198
00:11:28,967 --> 00:11:34,592
Bostrom's three doors: partial, the logic works but the premises are unproven.

199
00:11:34,592 --> 00:11:36,899
A pixel grid in space: not seen.

200
00:11:37,366 --> 00:11:41,562
Holographic jitter of space: ruled out, for one model.

201
00:11:41,562 --> 00:11:45,291
Physics proves we cannot be simulated: disputed.

202
00:11:45,291 --> 00:11:47,933
We live in a simulation: unproven.

203
00:11:48,399 --> 00:11:49,319
The verdict.

204
00:11:49,319 --> 00:11:52,769
The simulation hypothesis is not a discovery.

205
00:11:52,769 --> 00:11:57,139
It is one of humanity's oldest questions, in new clothes.

206
00:11:57,139 --> 00:12:04,499
Plato's shadows, Zhuangzi's butterfly, Shankara's rope, the Gnostic craftsman, Descartes' demon.

207
00:12:04,966 --> 00:12:07,770
Every test so far has come back empty.

208
00:12:07,770 --> 00:12:08,508
No pixels.

209
00:12:08,508 --> 00:12:09,246
No glitch.

210
00:12:09,246 --> 00:12:11,533
No message from the programmer.

211
00:12:11,533 --> 00:12:13,820
But no proof against it either.

212
00:12:13,820 --> 00:12:17,804
That is exactly what makes it philosophy, not science.

213
00:12:17,804 --> 00:12:18,100
Yet.

214
00:12:18,566 --> 00:12:22,015
And maybe Bostrom said the wisest part himself.

215
00:12:22,015 --> 00:12:27,664
Even if it is a simulation, the world we see is still the only guide we have.

216
00:12:27,664 --> 00:12:30,746
The people you love are still real to you.

217
00:12:30,746 --> 00:12:31,700
Live like it.

218
00:12:32,166 --> 00:12:33,573
That is the case file.

219
00:12:33,573 --> 00:12:36,899
More ancient mysteries are waiting at the next stop.

220
00:12:37,366 --> 00:12:39,510
Subscribe so you do not miss it.

221
00:12:39,510 --> 00:12:43,127
And tell us in the comments: which door do you choose?

222
00:12:43,127 --> 00:12:44,399
One, two, or three?
